      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think what\u0027s happening is that if you were to follow the guide on https://www.usmobile.com/apn-download and create the APN through the settings app, you would still allow the default Verizon APNs to be used, whereas creating an XML entry with the `mvno_match_data\u003d\"BA01500000000000\" mvno_type\u003d\"gid\"` attributes prevents any APNs without those attributes, including the default Verizon APNs, from being used. In my testing, if I add the following APN in conjunction with the US Mobile specific one, then my mobile data works:\n\n```\n\u003capn carrier\u003d\"US Mobile\" carrier_id\u003d\"2641\" mcc\u003d\"311\" mnc\u003d\"480\" apn\u003d\"IMS\" type\u003d\"ia\" protocol\u003d\"IPV4V6\" roaming_protocol\u003d\"IPV6\" profile_id\u003d\"2\" modem_cognitive\u003d\"true\" mvno_match_data\u003d\"BA01500000000000\" mvno_type\u003d\"gid\" /\u003e\n```\n\nThis is basically one of the default Verizon APNs, except I add the MVNO match filters so that it gets activated alongside the US Mobile APN.\n\nI don\u0027t have a stock device handy, but I\u0027m guessing it\u0027ll use the default Verizon APNs here: https://android.googlesource.com/device/sample/+/android16-release/etc/apns-full-conf.xml#16900, because those are the only ones with a matching MCC+MNC.\n\nI\u0027ve sent an email to US Mobile support to see if they have any more information.",
      "parentUuid": "5c4e1b3d_79f35d87",
      "revId": "5216d728d9766ece2bda8926efde71918aa8182f",
      "serverId": "1ec6b3db-b2c1-4fa4-84a2-4c7efe89ba71"
    },
